 ],
 "literal": "Like the dark wing of a winter kite / wearing ragged clothes, clinging to the base of a jackfruit tree / thinking of myself, traveling to another land, my / languishing grief, suffering, and distress having seen / with bloody, beautiful anklet-wearing feet that destroyed herds of deer / with a head shining with beautiful gems of bright rays / a noble lord, a hunter with a strong bow / I worshipped him, to me who rose, placing his hand over mine, making me sit / a rich piece of white fatty meat like ghee / before his young attendants, who were lost on the forest path, quickly / arrived / swiftly / he himself, with fire made by friction, hastening, roasted it, and said 'eat this with your large, great kinfolk', and gave it / eating it like ambrosia, my burning hunger left / on the fragrant, cool mountain slope where good trees are dense / drinking the cool water of the waterfall upon the rock / as I began to depart quickly / 'to obtain rare, splendid, fine ornaments / other than this we have none, we are of the forest land', saying / the shining gemmed necklace worn on his chest / and the bracelets on his tightly fitting jointed forearm, he gave / 'Which country?' asking, even his country he did not say / 'Who are you?' asking, even his name he did not say / as others said on the way, I heard / not made of iron, the famed Tōṭṭi / that mountain which protects the beautiful tall hills / with sweet water like carved crystal / the lord of the dense mountain land, Nalli, he is indeed.",
 "translation": "Wearing clothes as ragged as the dark wings of a winter kite, I huddled at the base of a jackfruit tree, lost in thought, traveling to another land. Seeing my languishing grief, suffering, and distress, a noble lord—a hunter with a strong bow, wearing beautiful hero's anklets stained with the blood of deer he had slain, his head shining with bright-rayed gems—approached. When I rose to worship him, he gently pressed my hand to make me sit. Before his young attendants, who had lost their way on the forest path, could arrive, he swiftly kindled a fire with friction-wood, quickly roasted a rich piece of white fatty meat as soft as ghee, and gave it to me, saying, 'Eat this with your large family.' I ate it as if it were ambrosia, and my burning hunger vanished. After drinking the cool water of a waterfall cascading over rocks on the fragrant, cool, densely wooded mountain slope, I prepared to depart quickly. He said, 'We are people of the forest, and we have no other rare, splendid ornaments to give,' and took off the shining gemmed necklace from his chest and the bracelets from his tightly fitting forearm and gave them to me. When I asked, 'Which is your country?' he did not name his land. When I asked, 'Who are you?' he did not reveal his name. But as I traveled on, I heard others say that he is indeed Nalli, the lord of the dense mountains with sweet water clear as carved crystal, who protects the beautiful tall hills of the famed Tōṭṭi mountain, which is not made of iron.",
 "modern": "குளிர்காலத்துப் பருந்தின் கரிய சிறகைப் போன்ற கிழிந்த ஆடையை உடுத்தி, பலா மரத்தின் அடியில் ஒதுங்கி, வறுமையால் வாடி வேறு நாடு நோக்கிச் சென்ற என் துன்பத்தையும் மெலிவையும் ஒரு வேடன் கண்டான். அவன் மான்களைக் கொன்ற குருதி படிந்த வீரக்கழல் அணிந்த கால்களையும், ஒளிவீசும் மணிகள் பதித்த முடியையும் உடைய ஒரு தலைவன் ஆவான். நான் அவனைத் தொழ முற்பட்டபோது, அவன் என் கையைப் பிடித்து அமரச் செய்தான். காட்டில் வழிதவறிய அவனது ஏவலர்கள் விரைந்து வந்து சேர்வதற்கு முன்னரே, அவன் தானே ஞெலிகோலால் தீயை உண்டாக்கி, நெய்யைப் போன்ற கொழுத்த இறைச்சித் துண்டுகளைச் சுட்டு, 'உன் சுற்றத்தாருடன் தின்பாயாக' என்று தந்து என் பசியைப் போக்கினான். மரம் செறிந்த குளிர்ந்த மலச்சாரலில் பாறை மேல் விழும் அருவிநீரைப் பருகி நான் புறப்படத் தயாரானபோது, 'நாங்கள் காட்டில் வாழ்பவர்கள், தருவதற்கு வேறு அரிய அணிகலன்கள் எங்களிடம் இல்லை' என்று கூறி, தன் மார்பில் அணிந்திருந்த முத்துவடத்தையும், கையில் அணிந்திருந்த கடகத்தையும் எனக்குக் கொடுத்தான். 'உமது நாடு எது?' என்று கேட்டபோது அவன் தன் நாட்டைச் சொல்லவில்லை. 'நீர் யார்?' என்று கேட்டபோது தன் பெயரையும் சொல்லவில்லை. ஆனால், வழியில் போவோர் கூறக் கேட்டு அவன் இரும்பால் செய்யப்படாத கோட்டையைக் கொண்ட தோட்டி மலையைக் காக்கும், பளிங்கு போன்ற தெளிந்த நீரையுடைய நளிமலை நாடனான நள்ளி என்று அறிந்து கொண்டேன்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is in the Paṭāṇ tiṇai and belongs to the Iyanmoli turai, where the poet praises the natural generosity of a patron. The poet, Van Paranar, describes his encounter with a hunter in the forest who relieved his extreme hunger and gifted him precious ornaments. The hunter did not reveal his name or country, showing extreme humility and selfless giving. Only later, by listening to travelers on the way, did the poet discover that the generous hunter was actually Kandeerakko Perunalli, the lord of the dense mountain region of Nalli. The imagery of the cold winter kite's wings represents the poet's extreme poverty, which is contrasted with the warmth of the freshly roasted meat and the cool, crystal-clear mountain water. Key words like 'இழுதின் அன்ன வால்நிணக் கொழுங்குறை' (rich white fatty meat like ghee) and 'நாடும் சொல்லான் பேரும் சொல்லான்' (did not say his country or name) highlight the chieftain's spontaneous and anonymous benevolence.",
